{
  "id": "calculus",
  "title": "Calculus & Optimization",
  "weightage": "3–6 marks",
  "summary": "Single-variable calculus only: typically 2–4 questions on limits (L'Hospital/Taylor), continuity/differentiability of piecewise or |x| functions, and local/global extrema on intervals, often as MSQs about properties of f with f''>0.",
  "notesGuide": "1) Print Paul's Online Math Notes Calculus I pages for Limits, Continuity, Definition of the Derivative, Critical Points, Min/Max Values, Finding Absolute Extrema, Shape of a Graph I & II and Optimization (each page also links a free PDF version and practice problems with solutions). 2) Download MIT 18.01 (Fall 2006) lecture notes and 18.01SC Unit 2 (Applications of Differentiation) and Unit 5 Part B (Taylor Series) session notes/problem sets. 3) Read MML book Ch 5.1 (Taylor series, differentiation) and Ch 7.1 (gradient descent / optimization) for the ML angle. 4) Make a 2-page sheet: standard limits (sin x/x, (1+x)^(1/x), (e^x-1)/x, ln(1+x)/x), Maclaurin series of e^x, sin, cos, ln(1+x), (1+x)^n to 3 terms, L'Hospital conditions, continuity vs differentiability checks (LHD/RHD), first/second-derivative tests, 'global extrema on [a,b] = check critical points AND endpoints', convexity facts (f''>0 => at most one critical point which is the global min). Add each PYQ trap.",
